---
import Article from '../../components/Article.astro';

const title = 'Euchre vs Spades: What’s the Difference? | CardHearth';
const description =
  'Euchre vs Spades compared: deck size, the bowers, bidding, going alone vs nil, and scoring — pick the partnership trick-taking game that fits you.';
const updated = '2026-06-14';
const heading = 'Euchre vs Spades';
const subtitle =
  'Two partnership trump games that feel very different — one short, sharp and bower-driven, the other a longer game of precise bidding. Here’s how they compare.';

const breadcrumb = [
  { name: 'CardHearth', href: '/' },
  { name: 'Guides', href: '/guides/' },
  { name: 'Euchre vs Spades', href: '/guides/euchre-vs-spades/' },
];

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Is Euchre faster than Spades?',
    a: 'Yes, noticeably. Euchre hands are only five tricks with a 24-card deck and a game runs to 10 points, so a full game is quick. Spades uses the full 52-card deck, 13 tricks a hand, and runs to 500 — a longer, more drawn-out game.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How is trump different in Euchre and Spades?',
    a: 'In Spades the trump suit is always spades. In Euchre a trump suit is chosen each hand, and two Jacks — the right and left bowers — become the top two trumps, which is unique to Euchre and changes how you value your hand.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What’s the difference between going alone and bidding nil?',
    a: 'Both are bold solo plays but opposite in aim. Going alone (Euchre) means playing without your partner to try to sweep all five tricks for 4 points. Bidding nil (Spades) means promising to win zero tricks for a 100-point swing. One chases everything; the other chases nothing.',
  },
];

const schema = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Article',
    headline: 'Euchre vs Spades: What’s the Difference?',
    description,
    datePublished: updated,
    dateModified: updated,
    author: { '@type': 'Person', '@id': 'https://cardhearth.com/#jared', name: 'Jared Waterworth', url: 'https://cardhearth.com/about/' },
    publisher: {
      '@type': 'Organization',
      name: 'CardHearth',
      url: 'https://cardhearth.com/',
      logo: { '@type': 'ImageObject', url: 'https://cardhearth.com/logo.png', width: 512, height: 512 },
    },
    mainEntityOfPage: 'https://cardhearth.com/guides/euchre-vs-spades/',
    image: 'https://cardhearth.com/og/euchre.png',
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: breadcrumb.map((c, i) => ({
      '@type': 'ListItem',
      position: i + 1,
      name: c.name,
      item: `https://cardhearth.com${c.href}`,
    })),
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    mainEntity: faq.map((f) => ({
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: f.q,
      acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
    })),
  },
];
---

<Article
  title={title}
  description={description}
  heading={heading}
  subtitle={subtitle}
  updated={updated}
  schema={schema}
  breadcrumb={breadcrumb}
  cta={{ href: '/euchre/', label: 'Play Euchre' }}
>
  <p>
    <a href="/euchre/">Euchre</a> and <a href="/spades/">Spades</a> are both four-player partnership
    games built on trump and trick-taking, so they're natural to compare. But one is a short, snappy
    game driven by two special Jacks, and the other is a longer game of careful bidding. Here's how
    they line up.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>At a glance</th><th>Euchre</th><th>Spades</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Deck</th><td>24 cards (9–Ace)</td><td>Full 52 cards</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Tricks per hand</th><td>5</td><td>13</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Trump</th><td>Chosen each hand, plus the bowers</td><td>Spades, always</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Bidding</th><td>Order up or name a suit</td><td>Bid exact tricks</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Solo play</th><td>Go alone (4 points)</td><td>Bid nil (±100)</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Game to</th><td>10</td><td>500</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Pace</th><td>Fast and punchy</td><td>Longer</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>What they share</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Four players in two fixed partnerships (you and the player opposite).</li>
    <li>A trump suit and follow-suit trick-taking.</li>
    <li>A bold solo option — going alone in Euchre, bidding nil in Spades.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The deck and hand length</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Euchre uses a 24-card deck</strong> — only the 9 through Ace of each suit — and deals
    <strong>five cards</strong> each, so a hand is just five tricks. <strong>Spades uses the full
    52-card deck</strong> and deals <strong>thirteen</strong>, so a hand is thirteen tricks. That
    alone makes Euchre much quicker per hand.
  </p>

  <h2>Trump: a chosen suit and the bowers</h2>
  <p>
    In <strong>Spades, trump is always spades</strong> — simple and fixed. In <strong>Euchre, trump
    is chosen each hand</strong> by ordering up or naming a suit, and the two Jacks become the
    powerhouse cards: the <strong>right bower</strong> (Jack of trump) is the highest card in the
    game, and the <strong>left bower</strong> (the same-colour Jack) becomes the second-highest trump.
    Learning to value the bowers is the heart of Euchre and has no equivalent in Spades.
  </p>

  <h2>Bidding</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Spades has precise bidding:</strong> every player names exactly how many tricks they
    expect, and your team is scored against that combined contract. <strong>Euchre's "bidding" is
    simpler:</strong> one team just commits to making trump and promises to take at least three of
    five tricks. Spades asks for an exact number; Euchre asks "can your team win the hand?"
  </p>

  <h2>Scoring and game length</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Euchre</strong> scores 1 point for taking 3–4 tricks, 2 for all five, and 4 for a lone
    sweep — and the first team to <strong>10</strong> wins, so games are short. <strong>Spades</strong>
    scores 10 per bid trick with bags and nil swings, running all the way to <strong>500</strong>.
    Euchre is a quick best-of; Spades is a longer campaign.
  </p>

  <h2>Which should you play?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Play Euchre</strong> if you want fast, punchy games, the unique bower mechanic, and
      quick momentum swings.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Play Spades</strong> if you enjoy precise bidding, longer games, and the deep
      partnership play of covering a nil.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>Both are free with a computer partner, so you can learn either in a few minutes.</p>

  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
  {
    faq.map((f) => (
      <details class="faq">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold">{f.q}</summary>
        <p class="mt-2 opacity-90">{f.a}</p>
      </details>
    ))
  }

  <h2>Keep going</h2>
  <p>
    Try them both: <a href="/euchre/">play Euchre</a> or <a href="/spades/">play Spades</a>. Learn the
    details in the <a href="/guides/euchre-rules/">Euchre rules</a> and
    <a href="/guides/spades-rules/">Spades rules</a>, compare the avoidance classic in
    <a href="/guides/hearts-vs-spades/">Hearts vs Spades</a>, or see all four in our
    <a href="/guides/best-free-card-games/">best free card games</a> roundup.
  </p>
</Article>
